{"content_hash": "c03e1d0abc9b54c8", "version_id": 173, "article_id": 18, "level_code": "en_b1", "lang": "en", "level": 3, "level_label": "B1 中级", "title": "Common Mouth Bacteria May Help Lungs Fight Pneumonia", "title_zh": "口腔里常见的细菌，可能帮肺抵抗肺炎", "topic": "health", "source": "medical_x", "source_url": "https://medicalxpress.com/news/2026-10-common-airway-bacterium-lungs-pneumonia.html", "image": {"type": "photo", "url": "https://scx1.b-cdn.net/csz/news/tmb/2024/lung-1.jpg", "credit": ""}, "published_date": "2026-10-07", "word_count": 259, "reading_minutes": 1.3, "lead": "A new study from the University of Colorado Anschutz found that a common bacterium in the mouth and airways may help the lungs fight pneumonia. The research was done in mice, but it may lead to new ways to protect people.", "lead_zh": "科罗拉多大学安舒茨分校的一项新研究发现，口腔和气道里常见的一种细菌，可能帮助肺部抵抗肺炎。研究是在小鼠身上做的，但也许能为保护人类找到新办法。", "preview": "Researchers at the University of Colorado Anschutz have found that a common bacterium may help the lungs defend themselves. The bacterium, c", "preview_zh": "科罗拉多大学安舒茨分校的研究人员发现，一种常见的细菌可能帮助肺部自我防御。这种细菌叫黑色素普雷沃菌，生活在健康人的口腔和气道里。这项研究发表在《美国国家科学院院刊》上。科学家想弄明白，为什么有些人得肺炎后病情比别人轻。", "has_audio": true, "audio": {"id": 163, "url": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/chjam12301-sys/news-etl@ab49ecc/content/audio/18/en_b1.mp3", "duration": 103.338, "size_bytes": 623808, "engine": "edge-tts", "voice": "en-US-AriaNeural", "word_count": 286, "has_timeline": true}, "detail_url": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/chjam12301-sys/news-etl@ab49ecc/content/data/versions/173.json", "paragraphs": ["Researchers at the University of Colorado Anschutz have found that a common bacterium may help the lungs defend themselves. The bacterium, called Prevotella melaninogenica, lives in the mouth and airways of healthy people. The study was published in the journal Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences. The scientists wanted to understand why some people get less sick from pneumonia than others.", "In the study, the team exposed mice to P. melaninogenica and later to Streptococcus pneumoniae, a leading cause of bacterial pneumonia. Because of the earlier exposure, the mice's immune cells were better able to recognize and clear the harmful bacteria. The mice also had much less pneumonia-causing bacteria in their lungs. The researchers used a method called single-cell RNA sequencing to see how the cells changed.", "The results showed that Prevotella exposure reprogrammed several types of immune cells, including neutrophils and macrophages. These cells were present in greater numbers and did a better job of killing the bacteria. Sarah Clark, the senior author, said the lungs meet bacteria from the mouth all the time, and these meetings may help educate the immune system. She added that this could open new ways to prevent respiratory infections.", "The study adds to growing evidence that bacteria in the respiratory tract are not just passive passengers. Earlier clinical studies have linked higher levels of Prevotella and more diverse airway bacteria with better outcomes in people with severe respiratory illness. However, the new research was done in mice, so more studies are needed to see if the same effect happens in humans."], "paragraphs_zh": ["科罗拉多大学安舒茨分校的研究人员发现，一种常见的细菌可能帮助肺部自我防御。这种细菌叫黑色素普雷沃菌，生活在健康人的口腔和气道里。这项研究发表在《美国国家科学院院刊》上。科学家想弄明白，为什么有些人得肺炎后病情比别人轻。", "研究中，团队先让小鼠接触黑色素普雷沃菌，之后再让它们接触肺炎链球菌——细菌性肺炎的主要病因之一。因为之前接触过普雷沃菌，小鼠的免疫细胞能更好地识别并清除有害细菌。小鼠肺里的肺炎致病菌也少了很多。研究人员用一种叫单细胞RNA测序的方法，观察细胞发生了哪些变化。", "结果显示，接触普雷沃菌会重新“编程”几种免疫细胞，包括中性粒细胞和巨噬细胞。这些细胞数量更多，杀灭细菌的能力也更强。资深作者莎拉·克拉克说，肺每天都会遇到来自口腔的细菌，这些接触也许能“教育”免疫系统。她还说，这可能为预防呼吸道感染开辟新途径。", "这项研究进一步证明，呼吸道里的细菌并不只是被动的“乘客”。此前的临床研究已经发现，普雷沃菌水平更高、气道细菌更多样的人，在患严重呼吸道疾病时结果更好。不过，这项新研究是在小鼠身上做的，所以还需要更多研究，才能知道同样的效果是否也会发生在人身上。"], "body": "Researchers at the University of Colorado Anschutz have found that a common bacterium may help the lungs defend themselves. The bacterium, called Prevotella melaninogenica, lives in the mouth and airways of healthy people. The study was published in the journal Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences. The scientists wanted to understand why some people get less sick from pneumonia than others.\n\nIn the study, the team exposed mice to P. melaninogenica and later to Streptococcus pneumoniae, a leading cause of bacterial pneumonia. Because of the earlier exposure, the mice's immune cells were better able to recognize and clear the harmful bacteria. The mice also had much less pneumonia-causing bacteria in their lungs. The researchers used a method called single-cell RNA sequencing to see how the cells changed.\n\nThe results showed that Prevotella exposure reprogrammed several types of immune cells, including neutrophils and macrophages. These cells were present in greater numbers and did a better job of killing the bacteria. Sarah Clark, the senior author, said the lungs meet bacteria from the mouth all the time, and these meetings may help educate the immune system. She added that this could open new ways to prevent respiratory infections.\n\nThe study adds to growing evidence that bacteria in the respiratory tract are not just passive passengers. Earlier clinical studies have linked higher levels of Prevotella and more diverse airway bacteria with better outcomes in people with severe respiratory illness. However, the new research was done in mice, so more studies are needed to see if the same effect happens in humans.", "body_zh": "科罗拉多大学安舒茨分校的研究人员发现，一种常见的细菌可能帮助肺部自我防御。这种细菌叫黑色素普雷沃菌，生活在健康人的口腔和气道里。这项研究发表在《美国国家科学院院刊》上。科学家想弄明白，为什么有些人得肺炎后病情比别人轻。\n\n研究中，团队先让小鼠接触黑色素普雷沃菌，之后再让它们接触肺炎链球菌——细菌性肺炎的主要病因之一。因为之前接触过普雷沃菌，小鼠的免疫细胞能更好地识别并清除有害细菌。小鼠肺里的肺炎致病菌也少了很多。研究人员用一种叫单细胞RNA测序的方法，观察细胞发生了哪些变化。\n\n结果显示，接触普雷沃菌会重新“编程”几种免疫细胞，包括中性粒细胞和巨噬细胞。这些细胞数量更多，杀灭细菌的能力也更强。资深作者莎拉·克拉克说，肺每天都会遇到来自口腔的细菌，这些接触也许能“教育”免疫系统。她还说，这可能为预防呼吸道感染开辟新途径。\n\n这项研究进一步证明，呼吸道里的细菌并不只是被动的“乘客”。此前的临床研究已经发现，普雷沃菌水平更高、气道细菌更多样的人，在患严重呼吸道疾病时结果更好。不过，这项新研究是在小鼠身上做的，所以还需要更多研究，才能知道同样的效果是否也会发生在人身上。", "text": "Researchers at the University of Colorado Anschutz have found that a common bacterium may help the lungs defend themselves. The bacterium, called Prevotella melaninogenica, lives in the mouth and airways of healthy people. The study was published in the journal Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences. The scientists wanted to understand why some people get less sick from pneumonia than others. In the study, the team exposed mice to P. melaninogenica and later to Streptococcus pneumoniae, a leading cause of bacterial pneumonia. Because of the earlier exposure, the mice's immune cells were better able to recognize and clear the harmful bacteria. The mice also had much less pneumonia-causing bacteria in their lungs. The researchers used a method called single-cell RNA sequencing to see how the cells changed. The results showed that Prevotella exposure reprogrammed several types of immune cells, including neutrophils and macrophages. These cells were present in greater numbers and did a better job of killing the bacteria. Sarah Clark, the senior author, said the lungs meet bacteria from the mouth all the time, and these meetings may help educate the immune system. She added that this could open new ways to prevent respiratory infections. The study adds to growing evidence that bacteria in the respiratory tract are not just passive passengers. Earlier clinical studies have linked higher levels of Prevotella and more diverse airway bacteria with better outcomes in people with severe respiratory illness. However, the new research was done in mice, so more studies are needed to see if the same effect happens in humans.", "vocab": [{"word": "bacterium", "pos": "noun", "zh": "细菌", "note": "复数形式是 bacteria，常用复数"}, {"word": "airway", "pos": "noun", "zh": "气道，呼吸道", "note": "常用复数 airways，指口鼻到肺的通道"}, {"word": "pneumonia", "pos": "noun", "zh": "肺炎", "note": "不可数名词，发音 /nuːˈmoʊniə/"}, {"word": "immune system", "pos": "phrase", "zh": "免疫系统", "note": "身体抵抗疾病的系统"}, {"word": "expose", "pos": "verb", "zh": "使接触，暴露于", "note": "常用 expose sb to sth 结构"}, {"word": "reprogram", "pos": "verb", "zh": "重新编程，改变原有功能", "note": "这里指改变免疫细胞的行为方式"}, {"word": "neutrophil", "pos": "noun", "zh": "中性粒细胞", "note": "一种白细胞，帮助杀灭细菌"}, {"word": "macrophage", "pos": "noun", "zh": "巨噬细胞", "note": "一种能吞噬细菌的免疫细胞"}, {"word": "respiratory", "pos": "adj", "zh": "呼吸的，呼吸道的", "note": "如 respiratory tract 呼吸道"}, {"word": "clinical study", "pos": "phrase", "zh": "临床研究", "note": "以人为对象的研究，区别于动物实验"}], "title_original": "Common airway bacterium may help prepare lungs to fight pneumonia", "summary_original": "A common bacterium found in the mouth and airways may help the lungs better defend themselves against pneumonia, according to new research from the University of Colorado Anschutz.", "body_original": "Common airway bacterium may help prepare lungs to fight pneumonia\nby\nUniversity of Colorado Anschutz\nedited by\nRobert Egan\nEditors' notes\nThis article has been reviewed according to Science X's\neditorial process\nand\npolicies\n.\nEditors\nhave highlighted\nthe following attributes while ensuring the content's credibility:\nfact-checked\npeer-reviewed publication\ntrusted source\nproofread\nThe GIST\nAdd to Preferred Sources\nA common bacterium found in the mouth and airways may help the lungs better defend themselves against pneumonia, according to new research from the University of Colorado Anschutz.\nThe study, published in\nProceedings of the National Academy of Sciences\n, found that exposure to Prevotella melaninogenica (P. melaninogenica), a type of bacterium commonly found in healthy airways, changed the behavior of immune cells in the lungs of mice. When the mice were later exposed to Streptococcus pneumoniae, a leading cause of bacterial pneumonia, their immune systems were better able to recognize, engulf and clear the bacteria.\nThe findings offer new insight into why some people are more resilient to respiratory infections than others and could eventually point toward new ways of strengthening the lungs' natural defenses against pneumonia.\n\"Our lungs encounter bacteria from our mouths and upper airways all the time, and those exposures may actually be helping to educate the immune system,\" said Sarah Clark, Ph.D., senior author and associate professor at the University of Colorado Anschutz School of Medicine. \"We found that exposure to P. melaninogenica changes the way immune cells respond, essentially putting them in a better position to fight a bacterial infection.\"\nPreparing the immune system\nStreptococcus pneumoniae is a major cause of pneumonia, but exposure to the bacterium does not always lead to serious illness. Researchers have increasingly suspected that the community of microorganisms living throughout the airway, known as the respiratory microbiome, may play an important role in determining how well someone fights an infection.\nPrevotella is one of the most common groups of bacteria found in the healthy mouth and respiratory tract. Previous research has found that higher levels of Prevotella are associated with better outcomes in people with respiratory infections.\nBut scientists did not know exactly how the bacterium might help protect against pneumonia.\nTo investigate, Clark and her colleagues exposed mice to P. melaninogenica and then challenged them with S. pneumoniae. The researchers used\nsingle-cell RNA sequencing\nto examine thousands of individual cells in the lungs and determine how their activity changed.\nThe results showed that Prevotella exposure essentially reprogrammed several types of immune cells involved in fighting infection.\nIn mice exposed to Prevotella, immune cells called neutrophils and macrophages were better at engulfing and killing S. pneumoniae. The mice also had significantly lower amounts of the pneumonia-causing bacteria in their lungs.\n\"The immune cells were present in greater numbers and behaving differently,\" Clark said. \"They had been functionally reprogrammed to do a better job of clearing bacteria.\"\nThe lung microbiome may be part of the body's first line of defense\nThe study adds to growing evidence that bacteria normally found in the respiratory tract are not simply passive passengers. Instead, they may actively influence how the immune system responds to disease.\nThe researchers note that clinical studies have linked greater respiratory microbial diversity and the presence of anaerobic bacteria such as Prevotella with better outcomes in people with severe respiratory illness.\nThe new findings provide a possible biological explanation for those observations.\n\"We're interested in understanding whether we can harness this natural interaction between the microbiome and the immune system,\" Clark said. \"If we can figure out how these bacteria educate immune cells to respond more effectively, it could open up entirely new approaches to preventing respiratory infections.\"\nPublication details\nSara N. Stoner et al, Myeloid cell reprogramming drives enhanced defense against Streptococcus pneumoniae lung infection following exposure to commensal Prevotella,\nProceedings of the National Academy of Sciences\n(2026).\nDOI: 10.1073/pnas.2623525123\nJournal information:\nProceedings of the National Academy of Sciences\nKey medical concepts\nStreptococcus pneumoniae\nPneumonia, Bacterial\nMacrophages\nNeutrophils\nClinical categories\nPulmonary medicine\nAllergy and immunology\nCommon illnesses & Prevention\nInfectious diseases\nProvided by\nUniversity of Colorado Anschutz\nWho's behind this story?\nRobert Egan\nBachelor's in mathematical biology, Master's in creative writing. Well-traveled with unique perspectives on science and language.\nFull profile →\nCitation\n:\nCommon airway bacterium may help prepare lungs to fight pneumonia (2026, October 7)\nretrieved 7 October 2026\nfrom https://medicalxpress.com/news/2026-10-common-airway-bacterium-lungs-pneumonia.html\nThis document is subject to copyright. Apart from any fair dealing for the purpose of private study or research, no\npart may be 